# Task 0 (supervisor, 2026-08-28): repo-wide default — every file git detects as text
# is normalized to LF in the repo AND checked out as LF. The specific rules below
# predate this line and stay authoritative where they overlap (last match wins).
* text=auto eol=lf

# Line endings are pinned to LF, and for one file type it is not a style preference.
#
# `src/modules/channels/voice-livekit/prompts/__fixtures__/*.txt` hold the system prompt exactly as
# it was before the persona refactor, and a test asserts `buildSystemPrompt()` reproduces them BYTE
# FOR BYTE. That assertion is what lets us change the prompt's structure without re-running the
# voice A/B suite on a live agent.
#
# With `core.autocrlf=true` — the default on Windows, and set on this machine — a fresh checkout
# rewrites those .txt files to CRLF. The generated prompt does NOT become uniformly CRLF to match:
# newlines inside template literals would follow the source file, but the `\n` escapes in
# `renderFaq()` and the objection-playbook slot are LF whatever the file's encoding. The result is a
# mixed-newline string compared against a uniformly-CRLF fixture, so the test fails on a clean clone
# while passing for everyone who already had the working copy — the worst shape a failure can take.
#
# It is also a production concern, not only a test one: a CRLF system prompt is not the prompt that
# was measured.
*.ts   text eol=lf
*.tsx  text eol=lf
*.js   text eol=lf
*.mjs  text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.sql  text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.txt  text eol=lf

# Shell scripts and Dockerfiles break outright with CRLF.
*.sh   text eol=lf
Dockerfile* text eol=lf

*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.wav  binary
*.mp3  binary
